{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=Otago and Canterbury}}
'''Waitaki''' is an electorate for the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]] that crosses the boundary of [[Otago|North Otago]] and [[Canterbury, New Zealand|South Canterbury]] towns on the East Coast of the [[South Island]].  The electorate was first established for the {{NZ election link|1871}} that determined the [[5th New Zealand Parliament]].  It has been abolished and re-established several times and in its early years was a two-member electorate for two parliamentary terms.  The current electorate has existed since the {{NZ election link|2008}} and is held by [[Miles Anderson (politician)|Miles Anderson]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].

==Population centres==
Through an amendment in the Electoral Act in 1965, the number of electorates in the [[South Island]] was fixed at 25, an increase of one since the 1962 electoral redistribution.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=108, 111, 112}} It was accepted that through the more rapid population growth in the [[North Island]], the number of its electorates would continue to increase, and to keep proportionality, three new electorates were allowed for in the 1967 electoral redistribution for the next election.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}} In the North Island, five electorates were newly created and one electorate was reconstituted while three electorates were abolished.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=107, 111}} In the South Island, three electorates were newly created and one electorate was reconstituted while three electorates were abolished (including Waitaki).{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=108, 112}} The overall effect of the required changes was highly disruptive to existing electorates, with all but three electorates having their boundaries altered.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=111f}} These changes came into effect with the {{NZ election link|1969}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Waitaki.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
This current Waitaki electorate is the successor to parts of the old [[Otago (New Zealand electorate)|Otago electorate]], with parts of central Otago moving into [[Clutha-Southland]], and the boundary extended far up the South Canterbury coast, to just outside [[Timaru]]. The electorate was last re-established for the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]]. The [[2006 New Zealand census|2006 census]] showed that there has been a general northwards population movement. Even though the number of South Island electorates is fixed, the decline in the population of electorates from [[Rakaia]] south has resulted in the boundaries of electorates from [[Invercargill]] north to Rakaia shifting northwards. However, Waitaki ended up over quota in the [[2013 New Zealand census|2013 census]] and redistribution resulted in all communities south of and including [[Herbert, New Zealand|Herbert]] being ceded to [[Dunedin North (New Zealand electorate)|Dunedin North]].<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |date=4 April 2014}}</ref> Waitaki contracted again in the 2020 redistribution, gaining the [[Waitati]] area from {{NZ electorate link|Dunedin North}}, but losing a large section of land around Alexandra to {{NZ electorate link|Southland}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref>

The largest town in the electorate is [[Oamaru]] (pop. {{NZ population data 2018|Oamaru|y}}). Other towns include [[Geraldine, New Zealand|Geraldine]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Geraldine|y}}), [[Twizel]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Twizel|y}}), [[Wānaka]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Wanaka|y}}), [[Waimate]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Waimate|y}}) and [[Cromwell, New Zealand|Cromwell]] ({{NZ population data 2018|Cromwell|y}})

==History==
The Waitaki electorate has existed four times: in [[1871 New Zealand general election|1871]] to [[1946 New Zealand general election|1946]];{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=166}} in [[1957 New Zealand general election|1957]] to [[1969 New Zealand general election|1969]]; in [[1978 New Zealand general election|1978]] to [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]] and lastly since {{NZ election link year|2008}}.

The first election in the electorate was contested by [[William Steward (New Zealand politician)|William Steward]] and Macassey in {{NZ election link year|1871}}, with Steward being successful.<ref>{{cite news|title=Waitaki District |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=NOT18710207.2.10.1 |access-date=21 November 2010 |work=North Otago Times |volume=XV |issue=597 |date=7 February 1871 |page=2}}</ref>

The next election was held in early January 1876. Waitaki had become a two-member electorate, and four candidates put their names forward. Steward and Joseph O'Meagher contested the election as abolitionists (i.e. they were in favour of abolishing the [[Provinces of New Zealand|provincial government]]), while [[Thomas William Hislop]] and [[Samuel Shrimski]] were provincialists (i.e. they favoured the retention of provincial government).<ref>{{cite news |title=Latest Telegrams |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=ST18751227.2.11 |access-date=21 November 2010 |work=[[The Southland Times]] |date=27 December 1875 |issue=2247 |page=2}}</ref> The provincialists won the election by quite some margin.<ref>{{cite news |title=Waitaki Election. Declaration of the Poll. |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=NOT18760112.2.13 |access-date=21 November 2010 |work=North Otago Times |date=12 January 1876 |volume=XXIII |issue=1170 |page=2}}</ref>

Hislop and Shrimski were both confirmed in the {{NZ election link|1879}},{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|pp=114, 139}} but Hislop resigned on 28 April 1880 "for private reasons".{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=114}}{{sfn|Cyclopedia Company Limited|1897|p=83 }} The resulting {{By-election link|Waitaki|1880}} was won by [[George Jones (New Zealand politician)|George Jones]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=117}}

From 1881 onwards, the electorate became a single-member constituency again.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=166}} [[Tom Duncan (politician)|Tom Duncan]] won the {{NZ election link|1881}} and the two subsequent elections.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=194}} In the {{NZ election link|1887}}, Duncan was opposed by John Reid,<ref>{{cite news|title=Waitaki|url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=TS18870916.2.10.13|access-date=19 May 2012|work=[[The Star (Christchurch)|The Star]]|date=16 September 1887|issue=6034|page=3}}</ref> but defeated him by 705 to 676 votes.<ref>{{cite news|title=Elections|url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=WCT18870928.2.10|access-date=19 May 2012|work=West Coast Times|date=28 September 1887|issue=6608|page=2}}</ref>  In the {{NZ election link|1890}}, Duncan successfully contested the [[Oamaru (New Zealand electorate)|Oamaru electorate]] instead,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=104}} with [[John McKenzie (New Zealand politician)|John McKenzie]] taking Waitaki. McKenzie had previously held [[Waihemo (New Zealand electorate)|Waihemo]] and went back to that electorate again for the {{NZ election link|1893}}.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=123}}

William Steward, who was the first representative of the electorate, had since 1881 represented [[Waimate (New Zealand electorate)|Waimate]]. He returned to Waitaki for the 1893 election, was successful and also won the five subsequent elections. He held the electorate until 1911. He was appointed to the [[New Zealand Legislative Council|Legislative Council]] in the following year, but died within months of the appointment.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|pp=86, 140}}

[[Francis Henry Smith]] succeeded Steward in the {{NZ election link|1911}}. At the next election in {{NZ election link year|1914}}, Smith stood unsuccessfully in the [[Timaru (New Zealand electorate)|Timaru electorate]]. The Waitaki electorate was won by [[John Anstey (politician)|John Anstey]] that year.  At the {{NZ election link|1919}}, Anstey was defeated by [[John Bitchener]], who held Waitaki until he was defeated in the {{NZ election link|1935}} by [[David Barnes (politician)|David Barnes]]. Barnes, in turn, held the electorate for one parliamentary term and was defeated in the {{NZ election link|1938}} by [[David Campbell Kidd]]. At the final count, Kidd had a majority of 10 votes, and Barnes applied for a magisterial recount; this increased the 1938 result to a majority of 14 votes.<ref>{{cite news |title=Waitaki Seat |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AS19381103.2.16 |access-date=9 November 2013 |work=[[Auckland Star]] |date=3 November 1938 |volume=LXIX |issue=260 |page=5}}</ref> Kidd represented Waitaki until 1946, when the electorate was abolished and he successfully stood in [[Waimate (New Zealand electorate)|Waimate]] instead.

Waitaki was re-established for the {{NZ election link|1957}} and was won by [[Thomas Hayman]], who had previously represented [[Oamaru (New Zealand electorate)|Oamaru]]. Hayman died in office on 2 January 1962 and was succeeded by [[Allan Dick (politician)|Allan Dick]], who won the [[1962 Waitaki by-election|1962 by-election]]. Dick held the electorate until 1969, when it was abolished again.

The electorate was re-established for the [[1978 New Zealand general election|1978 general election]]. [[Jonathan Elworthy]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] was the successful candidate. Elworthy was re-elected in the [[1981 New Zealand general election|1981 general election]], but defeated in the [[1984 New Zealand general election|1984 general election]] by [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]'s [[Jim Sutton]]. Sutton was re-elected in the [[1987 New Zealand general election|1987 general election]], but lost to National's [[Alec Neill]] in the [[1990 New Zealand general election|1990 general election]]. Neill was re-elected in the [[1993 New Zealand general election|1993 general election]]. At the end of the next term, in 1996, the electorate was abolished again. Neill failed to be selected by the National Party as a candidate for any of the electorates for the [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 general election]].

With the advent of [[Mixed-member proportional representation]] (MMP) voting system in 1996 and the resulting reduction in the number of constituencies, the electorate was split in half; the town of [[Oamaru]] was pulled into the resized [[Otago (New Zealand electorate)|Otago]] electorate and the balance was transferred into the new [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki]] electorate.

The Waitaki electorate was re-established for the {{NZ election link|2008}}, and [[Jacqui Dean]], incumbent since the {{NZ election link|2005}} in the {{NZ electorate link|Otago}} electorate won the election with a large majority against Labour's [[David Parker (New Zealand politician)|David Parker]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Jacqui Dean wins rejigged Waitaki seat in a landslide |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/southland-times/news/710876/Jacqui-Dean-wins-rejigged-Waitaki-seat-in-a-landslide |access-date=25 September 2014 |work=[[The Southland Times]] |date=9 November 2008}}</ref> Dean increased her majority in the {{NZ election link|2011}} against Labour's Barry Monks.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bruce |first1=David |title=Dean has eye on Cabinet post |url= http://www.odt.co.nz/election-2011/-south/188436/dean-has-eye-cabinet-post |access-date=25 September 2014 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=28 November 2011}}</ref> Dean was confirmed as the electorate's representative in the {{NZ election link|2014}}.<ref>{{cite news |title=Latest results: Waitaki |url= http://www.odt.co.nz/news/election-2014/316234/latest-results-waitaki |access-date=25 September 2014 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=20 September 2014}}</ref>

